DISCUSSION
The challenge file has been accidentally modified at the top, but we still have enough of the original script visible below. The key behavior is: initialize `loc.flag` with the contents of `flag.txt`, define `loc.hash = loc.flag+0x30`, then run many embedded commands that copy one flag byte into the hash area and tweak it. Since the displayed comment says the script is run as `r2 -i welcome2.r2 -q flag.txt`, the script likely validates `flag.txt` rather than printing the flag.

A fast way forward is to emulate the outer logic: treat the unknown 48-byte flag as input, apply each embedded command in hit order to a memory model, and require that the final hash area matches the initial hexdump bytes. To build that emulator, I first need all embedded commands in hit order, so I'll adjust the solver to parse the `hit*` offsets and dump each null-terminated command string located there.
